--TEST--
An inline snapshot of an object with a `secret` property renders it redacted, so neither the snapshot that holds nor the failure that does not carries the secret's bytes — ADR 0079 § 14, ADR 0092 § 5
--FILE--
<?nvs
// § 14's last sentence is the claim: a snapshot cannot become the place secrets
// get committed. It is not a rule this member enforces -- it is a consequence
// of the snapshot being `Core\Debug::render`'s text, which redacts a `secret`
// property off the declaring class. So the assertion is asked on both edges: a
// snapshot that holds, and a snapshot that fails, and neither side of either
// one may contain the value.

use Core\Test;

final class Vault {
    public secret string $token = "s3cr3t";
    public int $version = 1;
}

Vault $vault = new Vault();
// The `\$` is not incidental: a rendered property name begins with `$`, which
// a double-quoted literal reads as an interpolation, so an object's snapshot is
// written escaped. § 14's own example uses a `"""` block literal, which this
// language does not have -- `nvs_stdlib::test`'s doc comment records that the
// `--update` half is what would make this cost invisible.
string $rendering = "Vault#1 (2) {\n  \$token => [redacted]\n  \$version => int(1)\n}";

// 1. The rendering the snapshot is written against.
echo $rendering, "\n";

// 2. A snapshot equal to it holds, and the value never appears in it.
Core\Test::assertMatchesInline($vault, $rendering);
Test::assertTrue(!Core\Str::contains($rendering, "s3cr3t"));

// 3. The failing edge, from the empty snapshot § 14's workflow starts at: the
// failure quotes the rendering it produced, so the reader about to accept it is
// not handed the value either.
try {
    Test::assertMatchesInline($vault, "");
} catch (Core\Test\Failure $mismatch) {
    Test::assertTrue(!Core\Str::contains($mismatch->message, "s3cr3t"));
    Test::assertTrue(Core\Str::contains($mismatch->message, "[redacted]"));
    echo "the failure quotes the redaction and not the value\n";
}
--EXPECT--
Vault#1 (2) {
  $token => [redacted]
  $version => int(1)
}
the failure quotes the redaction and not the value
